# The frozen corpus is EVIDENCE. Its bytes must survive a checkout unchanged, or
# the SHA-256 recorded at capture time stops matching the file it names — and an
# integrity record that cannot be checked is worse than none.
#
# Git's default on Windows converts LF to CRLF on checkout. That would rewrite
# every captured HTML body and every headers file. `-text` disables all EOL
# conversion for this directory, so what was captured is what is restored.
case-study-01/corpus/** -text

# The exhibits are evidence too, and the same rule applies for the same reason: a
# SHA-256 taken at freeze time must still match after a checkout. This covers the
# source files read out of other repositories AND the built artefact, which is a
# rendered HTML file whose whitespace IS the exhibit — EOL conversion would edit
# the very bytes the defect lives in.
case-study-01/exhibits/** -text

# The compressed bodies behind items 11, 42 and 48 (runs/crawl/bodies-*.jsonl.br).
# Binary brotli: a single converted byte would corrupt the stream and every
# body in it, and the hashes of 394 observations would stop being checkable.
runs/crawl/*.br -text
